{"type":"message","id":"ec385b05","parentId":"31035d52","timestamp":"2026-10-06T11:26:45.532Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_IKXXYDaC25VV68VYhqm7tOb5|fc_055fb762f51dfcfc016ac4daf4aff087d095459ea1d04be7ae","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":" \n \n createS3Storage — production-grade storage on AWS S3 with signed-URL multipart, optional CDN fronting, and SSE.\n@@ -1099,7 +1099,7 @@\n \n # AI Resources\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/ai-resources\n \n \n Use filefn from your AI coding assistant — llms.txt, MCP server, Skills, and editor configurations.\n@@ -1139,7 +1139,7 @@\n \n # Claude\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/claude\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/ai-resources/claude\n \n \n Wire filefn into Claude Desktop and Claude Code — MCP server, CLAUDE.md, and recommended hints.\n@@ -1219,7 +1219,7 @@\n \n # Codex\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/codex\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/ai-resources/codex\n \n \n Wire filefn into OpenAI Codex / GPT-based agents — system prompt bundle, JSON schema, and tool wrappers.\n@@ -1317,7 +1317,7 @@\n \n # Cursor\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/cursor\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/ai-resources/cursor\n \n \n Wire filefn into Cursor — MCP server, project rules, and recommended .cursorrules.\n@@ -1388,7 +1388,7 @@\n \n # llms.txt\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/llms-txt\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/ai-resources/llms-txt\n \n \n The static AI surfaces — llms.txt, llms-full.txt, llms-rich.json — generated on every docs build.\n@@ -1487,7 +1487,7 @@\n \n # MCP server\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/mcp\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/ai-resources/mcp\n \n \n Connect AI assistants to live filefn docs via the Model Context Protocol — search, fetch, and reference.\n@@ -1640,7 +1640,7 @@\n \n # Skills\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/skills\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/ai-resources/skills\n \n \n Pre-baked filefn workflows your AI assistant can run on your behalf — install, configure, debug, extend.\n@@ -1698,7 +1698,7 @@\n \n # API Reference\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/api\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/api\n \n \n HTTP endpoints exposed by filefn — annotated OpenAPI spec covering uploads, downloads, share links, grants, processing, and capabilities.\n@@ -1717,7 +1717,7 @@\n \n # Core Concepts\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts\n \n \n How filefn is wired — upload sessions, multipart, policies, render intents, the canonical envelope/error model, share links, grants, dedup, offline, events, and security.\n@@ -1805,7 +1805,7 @@\n \n # Architecture\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/architecture\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/architecture\n \n \n How filefn separates upload sessions, files, share links, grants, processing, and adapters into composable subsystems.\n@@ -1917,7 +1917,7 @@\n \n # Artifacts\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/artifacts\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/artifacts\n \n \n How filefn models processing output — thumbnails, PDF previews, OCR text, transcoded video, audio waveforms — as first-class file rows.\n@@ -2031,7 +2031,7 @@\n \n # Deduplication\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/dedup\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/dedup\n \n \n Optional content-addressable storage with policy- and tenant-scoped checksums.\n@@ -2112,7 +2112,7 @@\n \n # Errors\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/errors\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/errors\n \n \n filefn's canonical error envelope and the full error code catalog.\n@@ -2214,7 +2214,7 @@\n \n # Events\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/events\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/events\n \n \n The canonical filefn event catalog — upload.started, part.recorded, file:uploaded, processing.*, file:deleted — with redaction rules.\n@@ -2296,7 +2296,7 @@\n \n # Grants\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/grants\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/grants\n \n \n Per-user / per-tenant permission grants with the four canonical capabilities — read, write, delete, share — and TTLs.\n@@ -2400,7 +2400,7 @@\n \n # Idempotency\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/idempotency\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/idempotency\n \n \n How filefn deduplicates upload-init calls and prevents accidental duplicate sessions.\n@@ -2483,7 +2483,7 @@\n \n # Multipart\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/multipart\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/multipart\n \n \n How filefn negotiates chunk size, total parts, and the two upload modes (signed-URL vs. proxy).\n@@ -2596,7 +2596,7 @@\n \n # Observability\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/observability\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/observability\n \n \n How filefn handles request IDs, structured logging, metrics, and secret redaction.\n@@ -2730,7 +2730,7 @@\n \n # Offline\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/offline\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/offline\n \n \n How @filefn/client stages uploads in OPFS, replays on reconnect, and serves pending-local previews.\n@@ -2842,7 +2842,7 @@\n \n # Policies\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/policies\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/policies\n \n \n Named upload policies that gate content type, size, visibility, storage target, and storage path layout.\n@@ -2979,7 +2979,7 @@\n \n # Rate limiting\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/rate-limiting\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/rate-limiting\n \n \n Per-route rate limits using @superfunctions/middleware on top of filefn's upload, download, share-download, and artifact-download endpoints.\n@@ -2993,9 +2993,10 @@\n \n ```ts\n const fileFn = createFileFn({\n-  db, storage,\n+  database: db, storage,\n+  stores: { atomicKv: redisAtomicStore },\n   rateLimit: {\n-    persistence: redisPersistence,        // or undefined for in-memory\n+    mode: \"strict\",\n     algorithm: \"sliding-window\",          // or \"fixed-window\" | \"token-bucket\"\n     limits: {\n       uploadInit:        { windowSeconds: 60, maxRequests: 10 },\n@@ -3043,21 +3044,23 @@\n - **`sliding-window`** — smooths out the boundary effect. Default for the production examples.\n - **`token-bucket`** — burst-friendly, refill over time. Best when you have legitimate bursty traffic (e.g. mass uploads from a script).\n \n-## Persistence\n-\n-In-memory persistence is fine for development and single-instance deployments. For multi-instance / Edge / load-balanced deployments, use a shared persistence:\n-\n-- Redis (recommended)\n-- Postgres\n-- Cloudflare KV\n-\n-`@superfunctions/middleware` ships persistence implementations or accepts your own.\n-\n-For a strict limit shared by multiple processes, configure `atomicStore` with\n-an `AtomicKVStoreAdapter` that implements `compareAndSet`. The limiter rejects\n-an `atomicStore` without CAS support instead of silently falling back to\n-process-local serialization. Plain `persistence` remains suitable for\n-single-process use or deployments that accept best-effort cross-replica limits.\n+## Shared stores and modes\n+\n+FileFn 0.2.0 uses `stores` and `rateLimit.mode`, not `rateLimit.persistence`.\n+\n+- **`strict`** — shared `stores.atomicKv` with `compareAndSet`; missing atomic\n+  storage is rejected instead of silently using process-local counters.\n+- **`best-effort`** — `stores.kv` for deployments that accept approximate\n+  cross-replica limits.\n+- **`local`** — in-process counters for development or a single instance.\n+\n+Without an explicit mode, FileFn selects strict for `stores.atomicKv`,\n+best-effort for `stores.kv`, otherwise local. Supply adapters implementing\n+the `@superfunctions/db` runtime-store contracts.\n+\n+The separate pre-built `rateLimiter` still accepts the middleware's own\n+`atomicStore` / `persistence` options.\n+\n \n ## What clients see\n \n@@ -3098,7 +3101,7 @@\n \n # Render intents\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/render-intents\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/render-intents\n \n \n How filefn returns the right artifact for a given UI surface — thumbnail, preview, full, or download — with placeholder fallback.\n@@ -3218,7 +3221,7 @@\n \n # Security\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/security\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/security\n \n \n filefn's threat model — what it protects, what it doesn't, and how to harden a deployment.\n@@ -3317,7 +3320,7 @@\n \n # Share links\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/share-links\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/share-links\n \n \n Tokenised, optionally auth-required, optionally download-capped public links to files.\n@@ -3426,7 +3429,7 @@\n \n # Storage targets\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/storage-targets\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/storage-targets\n \n \n Logical storage tiers (durable, temporary) and how to route originals and artifacts to different backends.\n@@ -3495,7 +3498,7 @@\n \n # Upload sessions\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/upload-sessions\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/upload-sessions\n \n \n The lifecycle of a single filefn upload — from init to complete, with every state transition and recovery hook.\n@@ -3614,7 +3617,7 @@\n \n # Visibility\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/visibility\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/visibility\n \n \n How filefn enforces public, private, and shared file access.\n@@ -3688,7 +3691,7 @@\n \n # Examples\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/examples\n \n \n End-to-end runnable filefn examples — full-stack demo and a production-shaped reference setup.\n@@ -3715,7 +3718,7 @@\n \n # Full demo\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples/full-demo\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/examples/full-demo\n \n \n Browser uploads, thumbnails, download / delete, smoke tests — Hono + SvelteKit + memory adapter + local FS storage.\n@@ -3801,7 +3804,7 @@\n \n # Production setup\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples/production\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/examples/production\n \n \n Multi-policy filefn server with auth, processing, S3 storage, and Postgres rows — production-shaped reference.\n@@ -3929,7 +3932,7 @@\n \n # Features\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/features\n \n \n Every bundled filefn feature, documented end to end — uploads, downloads, share links, grants, processing, dedup, quotas, offline, HEIC, policies, rate limiting.\n@@ -3964,7 +3967,7 @@\n \n # Dedup\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features/dedup\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/features/dedup\n \n \n Optional content-addressable storage with policy- and tenant-scoped checksums.\n@@ -4034,7 +4037,7 @@\n \n # Downloads\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features/downloads\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/features/downloads\n \n\n[649 more lines in file. Use offset=1097 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791286005532}}
