{"type":"message","id":"2b5ccd34","parentId":"e1faf7e8","timestamp":"2026-10-06T11:28:47.502Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_pDAp9rV4vU7jwjliQPpmoWeV|fc_055fb762f51dfcfc016ac4db6ea9e087d0b59ce788fbe6c65d","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6mNncm resolved=True devfn/docs/content/docs/getting-started.md\n<!-- cubic:v=1575d896-a107-4270-ac1c-f686aabc2b64 -->\n<!-- cubic:review-run=ce14b52f-6485-5594-8298-c25e4d334624 -->\n<!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":10} -->\nP1: `init` only previews and does not write a manifest, so the following `doctor --trust` fails with `DEVFN_CONFIG_NOT_FOUND`. Add `init --yes` after reviewing the preview and before continuing.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Prompt for AI agents</summary>\n\n```text\nCheck if this issue is valid — if so, understand the root cause and fix it. At devfn/docs/content/docs/getting-started.md, line 11:\n\n<comment>`init` only previews and does not write a manifest, so the following `doctor --trust` fails with `DEVFN_CONFIG_NOT_FOUND`. Add `init --yes` after reviewing the preview and before continuing.</comment>\n\n<file context>\n@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@\n+Install or invoke `@devfn/cli` from the repository you want to run. `init` previews detected configuration; review it before accepting generated files. The first load of any manifest needs `--trust`, even for JSON, because it can declare lifecycle commands. Trust is bound to the exact manifest digest, so an edit requires review and trust again.\n+\n+```sh\n+npx @devfn/cli init\n+npx @devfn/cli doctor --trust\n+npx @devfn/cli up --profile default\n</file context>\n```\n\n</details>\n\n```suggestion\nnpx @devfn/cli init\nnpx @devfn/cli init --yes\n```\n\n✅ Addressed in [`aabb7d6`](https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/commit/aabb7d6f0d3147d010d061f4a461ff1ba6d059a8)\n\n## PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6mNncp resolved=True devfn/docs/content/docs/reference/migration.md\n<!-- cubic:v=ad1673ee-4635-4324-9afd-65aded1f5e62 -->\n<!-- cubic:review-run=ce14b52f-6485-5594-8298-c25e4d334624 -->\n<!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":8} -->\nP1: This does not ensure DevFn uses the existing database volume: its worktree-specific Compose project can select a different project-scoped volume, and `down` removes DevFn-created containers, losing data stored only in their writable layers. Tell adopters to verify the intended volume and back up container-only data before the first start.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Prompt for AI agents</summary>\n\n```text\nCheck if this issue is valid — if so, understand the root cause and fix it. At devfn/docs/content/docs/reference/migration.md, line 15:\n\n<comment>This does not ensure DevFn uses the existing database volume: its worktree-specific Compose project can select a different project-scoped volume, and `down` removes DevFn-created containers, losing data stored only in their writable layers. Tell adopters to verify the intended volume and back up container-only data before the first start.</comment>\n\n<file context>\n@@ -0,0 +1,17 @@\n+5. Before the first start, back up every pre-existing file targeted by `environmentOutputs[].path`; DevFn overwrites configured outputs and cannot restore prior contents.\n+6. Start with a minimal profile and compare it with the existing manual startup flow.\n+7. Add full, OAuth, test, or docs profiles incrementally. Public tunnels must remain explicit.\n+8. Verify `devfn down` preserves databases and volumes before adopting it as the daily command.\n+\n+Rollback by running `devfn down`, then inspect `runtimeDir` and every `environmentOutputs[].path` in the manifest before removing generated files. The default generated location is `.devfn/`, but custom paths may live elsewhere. Preserve any target that existed before DevFn adoption; cleanup does not know whether a configured output replaced user-owned content. Finally remove the DevFn manifest and continue using the preserved original scripts. Docker volumes and source configuration are not deleted.\n</file context>\n```\n\n</details>\n\n```suggestion\n8. Before the first start, verify DevFn uses the intended database volume; its worktree-specific Compose project can select a new volume, and `devfn down` removes DevFn-created containers, losing data stored only in their writable layers.\n```\n\n✅ Addressed in [`13f5618`](https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/commit/13f561830279b4c40c8d095f2fdd38ff4c719bfa)\n\n## PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6mNncs resolved=True devfn/docs/src/app.css\n<!-- cubic:v=69eacb6c-f9aa-496f-8acc-a72ba3f731b6 -->\n<!-- cubic:review-run=ce14b52f-6485-5594-8298-c25e4d334624 -->\n<!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":8} -->\nP2: The sticky offset is disabled, so the docs sidebar and TOC can stick at the viewport top underneath the sticky TopBar. Enable the provided offset so both remain visible below it.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Prompt for AI agents</summary>\n\n```text\nCheck if this issue is valid — if so, understand the root cause and fix it. At devfn/docs/src/app.css, line 19:\n\n<comment>The sticky offset is disabled, so the docs sidebar and TOC can stick at the viewport top underneath the sticky TopBar. Enable the provided offset so both remain visible below it.</comment>\n\n<file context>\n@@ -0,0 +1,72 @@\n+   * TopBar is outside DocsLayout but uses position:sticky in theme.css.\n+   * Set this to roughly the rendered top bar height so the docs sidebar/TOC stick below it.\n+   */\n+  /* --docsfn-sticky-top-offset: 2.7rem; */\n+\n+  /* Whole docs chrome (left sidebar + article + right TOC) max width; centers as a group */\n</file context>\n```\n\n</details>\n\n✅ Addressed in [`13f5618`](https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/commit/13f561830279b4c40c8d095f2fdd38ff4c719bfa)\n\n## PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6mNnct resolved=True devfn/docs/content/docs/reference/configuration.md\n<!-- cubic:v=0cbacab6-a009-4bc2-81fc-0c594b050ee8 -->\n<!-- cubic:review-run=ce14b52f-6485-5594-8298-c25e4d334624 -->\n<!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":9} -->\nP2: This overstates validation: `prerequisites[].profiles` is not checked, so an unknown profile silently skips that diagnostic in `doctor`. Limit this claim to the reference fields the validator checks, or add the missing validation.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Prompt for AI agents</summary>\n\n```text\nCheck if this issue is valid — if so, understand the root cause and fix it. At devfn/docs/content/docs/reference/configuration.md, line 28:\n\n<comment>This overstates validation: `prerequisites[].profiles` is not checked, so an unknown profile silently skips that diagnostic in `doctor`. Limit this claim to the reference fields the validator checks, or add the missing validation.</comment>\n\n<file context>\n@@ -0,0 +1,72 @@\n+| `environmentOutputs` | no | Repository-relative dotenv or JSON runtime files and owner-only permission modes. |\n+| `policy` | no | Repository-relative organization policy JSON path. |\n+\n+Every repository-relative path rejects absolute paths, `..` escape, and runtime symlink escape. References to ports, processes, services, profiles, dependencies, and hostname targets must resolve during validation. An explicitly configured but missing policy is an error; only the conventional unconfigured policy path is optional.\n+\n+## Ports\n</file context>\n```\n\n</details>\n\n```suggestion\nEvery repository-relative path rejects absolute paths, `..` escape, and runtime symlink escape. References to ports, processes, services, dependencies, `defaultProfile`, hostname profile filters, and hostname targets must resolve during validation; prerequisite profile filters are not currently checked. An explicitly configured but missing policy is an error; only the conventional unconfigured policy path is optional.\n```\n\n✅ Addressed in [`13f5618`](https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/commit/13f561830279b4c40c8d095f2fdd38ff4c719bfa)\n\n## PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6mNncu resolved=True devfn/docs/content/docs/lifecycle.md\n<!-- cubic:v=f56220e7-196c-4b7e-b6fa-b1e625c3fa04 -->\n<!-- cubic:review-run=ce14b52f-6485-5594-8298-c25e4d334624 -->\n<!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":10} -->\nP2: This overstates where runtime artifacts live: machine registry state uses the platform state directory, and configured `environmentOutputs[].path` files can be elsewhere in the repository. Name only the artifacts stored under `runtimeDir` and call out those separate locations so users can find and clean up state safely.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Prompt for AI agents</summary>\n\n```text\nCheck if this issue is valid — if so, understand the root cause and fix it. At devfn/docs/content/docs/lifecycle.md, line 10:\n\n<comment>This overstates where runtime artifacts live: machine registry state uses the platform state directory, and configured `environmentOutputs[].path` files can be elsewhere in the repository. Name only the artifacts stored under `runtimeDir` and call out those separate locations so users can find and clean up state safely.</comment>\n\n<file context>\n@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@\n+\n+`devfn up` plans resources in dependency order, reserves ports before mutation, starts selected native processes and Compose services, verifies readiness, registers proxy routes, and returns a machine-readable receipt. If an invocation fails, rollback targets resources created by that invocation. `status`, `logs`, and `doctor` expose live state and diagnostics; `down` stops only the exact worktree instance.\n+\n+There is no background daemon in version 0.1. State, journals, logs, and generated environment files are kept under the configured runtime directory, `.devfn/` by default. Process ownership uses a PID birth signature where the host supplies one, so cleanup refuses a reused PID.\n+\n+Ordinary `down` does not delete persistent Docker volumes. It is also not a general reset command. Explicit configured environment outputs may have overwritten files that existed before adoption; inspect them before manual cleanup. The [core package](/docs/reference/core), [process supervisor](/docs/reference/processes), and [migration guide](/docs/reference/migration) describe lifecycle boundaries.\n</file context>\n```\n\n</details>\n\n```suggestion\nThere is no background daemon in version 0.1. Project receipts, journals, and logs are kept under the configured runtime directory, `.devfn/` by default; machine registry state uses the platform state directory, and configured environment outputs may be written elsewhere in the repository. Process ownership uses a PID birth signature where the host supplies one, so cleanup refuses a reused PID.\n```\n\n✅ Addressed in [`13f5618`](https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/commit/13f561830279b4c40c8d095f2fdd38ff4c719bfa)\n\n## PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6mNncv resolved=True devfn/docs/README.md\n<!-- cubic:v=128b4c96-8b66-4131-9b8a-b01ecd6330fa -->\n<!-- cubic:review-run=ce14b52f-6485-5594-8298-c25e4d334624 -->\n<!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":9} -->\nP2: The validation command cannot find the locally installed `docsfn` binary in a normal shell. Invoke it through `npx` so the workspace-local CLI runs.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Prompt for AI agents</summary>\n\n```text\nCheck if this issue is valid — if so, understand the root cause and fix it. At devfn/docs/README.md, line 12:\n\n<comment>The validation command cannot find the locally installed `docsfn` binary in a normal shell. Invoke it through `npx` so the workspace-local CLI runs.</comment>\n\n<file context>\n@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@\n+npm --workspace @devfn/docs run build\n+```\n+\n+The build regenerates `static/llms.txt` and `static/llms-full.txt`. Run `docsfn validate --root .` from `devfn/docs`. The site defaults to port 6017. Set `site.canonicalUrl` in `docsfn.config.ts` after the public host is assigned.\n</file context>\n```\n\n</details>\n\n✅ Addressed in [`13f5618`](https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/commit/13f561830279b4c40c8d095f2fdd38ff4c719bfa)\n\n## PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6mNncy resolved=True devfn/docs/content/docs/reference/security.md\n<!-- cubic:v=5b917587-442f-45c6-ad8a-57a0c8ed2280 -->\n<!-- cubic:review-run=ce14b52f-6485-5594-8298-c25e4d334624 -->\n<!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":9} -->\nP2: The lifecycle receipt serializes each managed process's `command` verbatim, so credentials passed as command arguments are persisted despite this guarantee. Limit the claim to `secretEnv` values and warn against embedding credentials in arguments.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Prompt for AI agents</summary>\n\n```text\nCheck if this issue is valid — if so, understand the root cause and fix it. At devfn/docs/content/docs/reference/security.md, line 9:\n\n<comment>The lifecycle receipt serializes each managed process's `command` verbatim, so credentials passed as command arguments are persisted despite this guarantee. Limit the claim to `secretEnv` values and warn against embedding credentials in arguments.</comment>\n\n<file context>\n@@ -0,0 +1,16 @@\n+# DevFn security\n+\n+- Every DevFn manifest can declare lifecycle commands, so the CLI refuses to load JSON, TypeScript, or JavaScript until the user records trust for its exact digest with `--trust`. JSON is parsed directly from the verified bytes; executable manifests run from a private copy of those same bytes and must be self-contained, with imports and `require()` rejected so dependency files cannot bypass the digest boundary.\n+- Runtime children inherit a small base environment plus explicit `envAllowlist` entries. Sensitive-looking keys must also be listed in `secretEnv`; a detached wrapper redacts those effective values before native-process logs are persisted. Literal values are rejected only when their key looks sensitive, so review every manifest literal. Compose logging is disabled for services with `secretEnv` because Docker otherwise retains historical unredacted logs. Secrets are not persisted in the registry or lifecycle receipt.\n+- Generated environment and state files use owner-only modes (default `0600`); their directories use `0700`. Existing files are explicitly re-permissioned after each write.\n\n[631 more lines in file. Use offset=211 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791286127502}}
