e":0,"total":0.0321672}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791366423836,"responseId":"resp_055fb762f51dfcfc016ac61518a15487d0b73e02b1e29c96b7","rawStopReason":"completed","thinkingLevel":"high"}}
{"type":"message","id":"99d6e620","parentId":"f078071c","timestamp":"2026-10-07T09:47:37.546Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_hubrKHpjcAv2su0bkHrhx1w8|fc_055fb762f51dfcfc016ac615393f7c87d088932ad4dff574ce","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n## 4. Use timestamps only as a secondary lens\n\nIf the user explicitly asks for findings since a push:\n\n1. resolve the exact pushed commit SHA\n2. read its absolute UTC timestamp\n3. use that timestamp only to describe which feedback arrived later\n\nStill include an older unresolved thread when it remains actionable on the current head.\n\n## 5. Classify root causes before editing\n\nKeep a compact working ledger keyed by root cause: thread and check/annotation IDs and URLs, snapshot SHA, affected paths, current-code evidence, status, and verification. Keep it in task context for a single pass; persist it in the reusable task handoff for recurring runs as described above.\n\nClassify every relevant finding as exactly one of:\n\n- `Actionable`: a current defect within scope that can be addressed in this round.\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`: current-code evidence shows the behavior is fixed, superseded, or no longer applicable. An outdated hunk alone is not proof; trace its current implementation.\n- `Invalid`: current-code or behavioral evidence disproves the reported defect. Record the evidence, not just disagreement with the reviewer.\n- `Accepted Risk`: a verified defect explicitly accepted by the user or an identified authorized decision-maker. Record the decision, scope, impact and follow-up. The agent cannot use this label to clear unfinished work; accepted risks remain visible and do not count as fixes.\n- `Not Attempted`: a blocked current defect, out-of-scope concern, or external/non-code gate. Distinguish these reasons. A fixable code, dependency-pin, or workflow-configuration finding from a failed check is not an external gate merely because CI reported it. A valid blocked defect remains unresolved; do not count it as clean.\n\nDeduplicate comments reporting the same root cause. An unchanged old comment is not evidence that its issue is fixed. Reuse prior verified evidence only when the relevant code and contract remain unchanged.\n\n## 6. Establish the affected contract\n\nFor each actionable root cause, before editing:\n\n1. State the behavior that must hold and the concrete failing case.\n2. Trace its producers, consumers, callers, and sibling implementations in the PR worktree. Inspect equivalent entrypoints such as CLI versus batch operations, gateway versus worker execution, and normal versus retry/recovery paths when relevant.\n3. Identify where the invariant is enforced and what the proposed change could break. For stateful changes, consider ownership, fencing, ordering, partial failure, and cleanup where they affect this contract.\n4. Record which paths need changes, which already satisfy the contract, and the behavioral evidence needed to verify the fix.\n5. Make assumptions and exclusions explicit. For security or serialization, identify the protected data, threat model, final consumer-visible boundary and permitted structural output. Resolve contradictory or impossible guarantees before implementation. A substring match, for example, needs analysis of data provenance and the promised artifact contract before being classified as disclosure.\n\nThe unit of remediation is the root cause and its affected contract, not the commented line. A directly related defect discovered during this inspection belongs to the same scoped batch even when no reviewer named that line. Record its connection to the snapshot finding. Unrelated discoveries remain out of scope.\n\n## 7. Implement the smallest complete repair\n\nRepair every applicable path identified above. Prefer a shared enforcement point when it simplifies correctness; do not introduce an abstraction solely to satisfy this procedure.\n\nPreserve unrelated dirty work and exclude unrelated untracked and `.conduct/` artifacts. Do not expand into repository-wide cleanup or redesign. If a complete repair requires expanded authority or an unavailable dependency, explain the remaining defect and classify it as blocked rather than presenting a symptom patch as complete.\n\n## 8. Verify behavior and review the fix batch\n\nFor each root cause, demonstrate the previously failing behavior and its corrected outcome with focused regression coverage where practical. Confirm the test would fail on the old behavior, by running it against the old code when safe or by explaining the distinguishing assertion. Do not claim a reproduced failure when it was only reasoned through.\n\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract. Verify consumer-visible outcomes at the relevant boundaries: serialized artifacts, cleanup recovery, supported package entrypoints, or export/replay. Exercise combinations and composition where failures arise from interacting inputs; use property-based or generated cases when justified, without requiring them for every small fix. A regression matrix must test the stated invariant, not merely enumerate examples from bot comments.\n\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract:\n\n- For SQL, claims, locks, and transactions, prove resulting state and relevant concurrency behavior using a real disposable database when feasible. SQL-text assertions alone do not establish correctness.\n- For retry/recovery, cover the relevant interrupted state and the evidence needed to declare success.\n- For configuration propagation, verify both the executing component's configuration and required secret wiring without deploying.\n- Tests that mutate fixtures must restore prior values and row existence or use isolated disposable fixtures.\n\nFor operating-system lifecycle, process ownership, cleanup, or resource-release findings, the acceptance oracle must inspect the external OS-visible state. Do not prove absence of processes with the same liveness helper being changed. When the defect depends on PID 1, signals, reparenting, process groups, namespaces, or platform cleanup, reproduce that environment faithfully and prove the old head fails the distinguishing assertion before accepting a repair.\n\nIf behavioral verification is unavailable, report the gap explicitly. Test counts, mocks that mirror the implementation, and typechecks are not substitutes for proof of the failing behavior.\n\nAfter editing, review the entire fix diff together with its callers and affected contract before staging. Check for missed sibling paths, newly broken consumers, error/cleanup regressions, and interactions between fixes. Re-run checks when justified by subsequent changes or unresolved concerns. Complete local repair and verification within this single pre-push batch.\n\nRun focused tests first, then proportionate typecheck, lint, test, and build gates. Distinguish change-caused failures, baseline/environment limits, local proof, and external state.\n\nDo not wait for external CI in this skill. The required pre-edit current-head check snapshot is part of finding collection; after the push, one immediate check snapshot is allowed only for reporting. No polling. Skipped, failed, or absent reviewers do not prove coverage.\n\n## 9. Commit and push the fixes exactly once\n\nInvoking this skill authorizes one reviewed commit and one normal push of the fixes produced by this round to the PR head branch. Do not leave completed fixes only in the local worktree. If the fetched snapshot contains no actionable findings and no changes were made, do not create an empty commit or push.\n\nBefore committing:\n\n1. review the exact staged allowlist\n2. run `git diff --cached --check`\n3. confirm unrelated artifacts are excluded\n4. confirm the root-cause ledger identifies evidence for every claimed fix and lists remaining valid defects\n5. confirm no reset trigger remains unaddressed and no claimed complete repair leaves a known affected path deferred\n\nCreate exactly one commit for the reviewed fix batch and push it once to the PR head branch. Do not force-push. Verify the local, remote, and PR head SHAs once after pushing.\n\nDo not refetch review feedback after the push. Do not wait for the pushed commit to receive new reviews.\n\n## 10. Reconcile the queue\n\nSeparate remaining code defects from open GitHub thread and failed-check counts. Report how many snapshot threads and check diagnostics are fixed/already fixed, still valid, blocked, or unassessed, distinguishing surface counts from deduplicated root causes.\n\nThis skill does not by itself authorize posting replies, resolving threads, or dismissing reviews. If separately authorized by the user, resolve only snapshot threads whose full concern is covered by current-code evidence and the verified PR head; use captured IDs and mutation responses without refetching feedback. Never resolve a valid blocked finding or dismiss a review merely to reduce the count. Otherwise report fixed threads as eligible for resolution while leaving their GitHub state unchanged.\n\n## 11. Report and terminate\n\nReport with these explicit buckets:\n\n- `Fixed`\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`\n- `Not Attempted`\n- `Verification`\n- `Invalid / Accepted Risk` when applicable\n- `Convergence`: valid blocking root causes before/after, newly discovered defects, fix-induced regressions, recurring families, oldest deferred blockers and verification gaps. Distinguish assessed from unassessed items. Use improving, stalled, regressing or unknown with evidence; more tests, commits or resolved threads alone do not establish progress.\n- `Convergence gate`: ledger path, stable family IDs, failed-round counts, mode (`routine`, `architecture_reset_required`, or `architecture_reset`), and the assessment fields used to open any reset gate.\n\nFor each fixed root cause, summarize affected paths and behavioral evidence. Include unverified contracts and valid blocked defects; a completed round does not establish merge readiness.\n\nInclude the exact pushed commit SHA and PR URL. If no changes were needed, say explicitly that no commit or push was created. State failed, pending, or unverified CI and reviewer checks without waiting for them. A local fix does not establish that a hosted quality gate has passed.\n\nEnd with a clear termination statement such as:\n\n> One review round is complete. No post-push refetch or additional review loop was performed.\n\nThen stop.\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791366457546}}
{"type":"message","id":"ba6ea97f","parentId":"99d6e620","timestamp":"2026-10-07T09:47:37.546Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_mQeZggnxyVbn32UxmMaty4e7|fc_055fb762f51dfcfc016ac615393f9887d09d3a0b848b8e875b","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"      \"engines\": {\n        \"node\": \"^18.0.0 || ^20.0.0 || >=22\"\n      },\n      \"peerDependencies\": {\n        \"@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte\": \"^5.0.0\",\n        \"svelte\": \"^5.0.0\",\n        \"vite\": \"^6.0.0\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"authfn/docs/node_modules/esbuild\": {\n    \"base\": null,\n    \"proposed\": {\n      \"version\": \"0.25.12\",\n      \"resolved\": \"https://registry.npmjs.org/esbuild/-/esbuild-0.25.12.tgz\",\n      \"integrity\": \"REDACTED/BrPetoa0oksYoKXoG4ryJg==\",\n      \"dev\": true,\n      \"hasInstallScript\": true,\n      \"license\": \"MIT\",\n      \"bin\": {\n        \"esbuild\": \"bin/esbuild\"\n      },\n      \"engines\": {\n        \"node\": \">=18\"\n      },\n      \"optionalDependencies\": {\n        \"@esbuild/aix-ppc64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/android-arm\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/android-arm64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/android-x64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/darwin-arm64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/darwin-x64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/freebsd-arm64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/freebsd-x64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/linux-arm\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/linux-arm64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/linux-ia32\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/linux-loong64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/linux-mips64el\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/linux-ppc64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/linux-riscv64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/linux-s390x\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/linux-x64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/netbsd-arm64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/netbsd-x64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/openbsd-arm64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/openbsd-x64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/openharmony-arm64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/sunos-x64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/win32-arm64\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/win32-ia32\": \"0.25.12\",\n        \"@esbuild/win32-x64\": \"0.25.12\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"authfn/docs/node_modules/fdir\": {\n    \"base\": null,\n    \"proposed\": {\n      \"version\": \"6.5.0\",\n      \"resolved\": \"https://registry.npmjs.org/fdir/-/fdir-6.5.0.tgz\",\n      \"integrity\": \"REDACTED\",\n      \"dev\": true,\n      \"license\": \"MIT\",\n      \"engines\": {\n        \"node\": \">=12.0.0\"\n      },\n      \"peerDependencies\": {\n        \"picomatch\": \"^3 || ^4\"\n      },\n      \"peerDependenciesMeta\": {\n        \"picomatch\": {\n          \"optional\": true\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"authfn/docs/node_modules/picomatch\": {\n    \"base\": null,\n    \"proposed\": {\n      \"version\": \"4.0.7\",\n      \"resolved\": \"https://registry.npmjs.org/picomatch/-/picomatch-4.0.7.tgz\",\n      \"integrity\": \"sha512-qcJu88Q2IWqJsDD529JKMdwGm/REDACTED\",\n      \"dev\": true,\n      \"license\": \"MIT\",\n      \"peer\": true,\n      \"engines\": {\n        \"node\": \">=12\"\n      },\n      \"funding\": {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/sponsors/jonschlinkert\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"authfn/docs/node_modules/vite\": {\n    \"base\": null,\n    \"proposed\": {\n      \"version\": \"6.4.3\",\n      \"resolved\": \"https://registry.npmjs.org/vite/-/vite-6.4.3.tgz\",\n      \"integrity\": \"REDACTED/pVyUJYWMws3jg21uTJw/REDACTED\",\n      \"dev\": true,\n      \"license\": \"MIT\",\n      \"peer\": true,\n      \"dependencies\": {\n        \"esbuild\": \"^0.25.0\",\n        \"fdir\": \"^6.4.4\",\n        \"picomatch\": \"^4.0.2\",\n        \"postcss\": \"^8.5.3\",\n        \"rollup\": \"^4.34.9\",\n        \"tinyglobby\": \"^0.2.13\"\n      },\n      \"bin\": {\n        \"vite\": \"bin/vite.js\"\n      },\n      \"engines\": {\n        \"node\": \"^18.0.0 || ^20.0.0 || >=22.0.0\"\n      },\n      \"funding\": {\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/vitejs/vite?sponsor=1\"\n      },\n      \"optionalDependencies\": {\n        \"fsevents\": \"~2.3.3\"\n      },\n      \"peerDependencies\": {\n        \"@types/node\": \"^18.0.0 || ^20.0.0 || >=22.0.0\",\n        \"jiti\": \">=1.21.0\",\n        \"less\": \"*\",\n        \"lightningcss\": \"^1.21.0\",\n        \"sass\": \"*\",\n        \"sass-embedded\": \"*\",\n        \"stylus\": \"*\",\n        \"sugarss\": \"*\",\n        \"terser\": \"^5.16.0\",\n        \"tsx\": \"^4.8.1\",\n        \"yaml\": \"^2.4.2\"\n      },\n      \"peerDependenciesMeta\": {\n        \"@types/node\": {\n          \"optional\": true\n        },\n        \"jiti\": {\n\n[744 more lines in file. Use offset=286 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791366457546}}
{"type":"message","id":"02560dff","parentId":"ba6ea97f","timestamp":"2026-10-07T09:47:37.546Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_4RjTrBKCsMuLoo9EmhB6DIOJ|fc_055fb762f51dfcfc016ac615393fa487d0b9d8a32e74ec6c35","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":" ```text\n-# authfn — Self-hosted authentication for any stack\n-\n-> Sessions, OTP, passwords, social OAuth, 2FA, API keys, multi-region...\n-\n-## Welcome\n-[full body of index.md]\n-\n-## Getting Started\n-[full body of getting-started.md]\n-\n-## Core Concepts › Architecture\n-[full body of architecture.md]\n-\n-...\n-\n-## OpenAPI\n-- POST /sign-up/password — Create a new account with email and password.\n-- POST /sign-in/password — Sign in with email and password.\n-[...all 31 operations...]\n-```\n-\n-## Customizing\n-\n-The `docs:llms` task is configurable via `docsfn.config.ts`:\n-\n-```ts\n-{\n-  // ...\n-  llmsTxt: {\n-    enabled: true,\n-    includeOpenApi: true,\n-    includePages: ['docs/**'],\n-    excludePages: ['docs/blog/**'],\n-  },\n-}\n-```\n-\n-## Token budget\n-\n-| File | Approximate size |\n-| --- | --- |\n-| `llms.txt` | ~8 KB (~2,000 tokens) |\n-| `llms-full.txt` | ~250 KB (~65,000 tokens) |\n-\n-For models with smaller context windows, prefer `llms.txt` and use the [MCP server](./mcp) for on-demand retrieval.\n+https://authfn.com/docs/llms-full.txt\n+```\n+\n+The default local docs port is 6005:\n+\n+```text\n+http://localhost:6005/docs/llms.txt\n+http://localhost:6005/docs/llms-full.txt\n+```\n+\n+## Configuration and size\n+\n+The manifest follows the configured content directories and `site.canonicalUrl` in `docsfn.config.ts`. The shared `scripts/docs-site/llms.mjs` builder passes the canonical URL and excludes Blog posts with the published `includeBlog:false` option. There is no consumer `docs:llms` script or invented `llmsTxt` configuration object.\n+\n+Generated sizes vary with the corpus and API schema. Prefer the short index for smaller context budgets; retrieve individual linked pages or use the separately configured [MCP server](./mcp) when appropriate. Static context files do not authenticate an assistant or establish a live MCP connection.\n \n ---\n \n--- reviewed42a/authfn/docs/static/llms.txt\n+++ current213/authfn/docs/static/llms.txt\n@@ -4,116 +4,116 @@\n \n ## Documentation\n \n-- [Custom database adapter](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/database/custom) — Implement the `@superfunctions/db` adapter contract for any storage backend.\n-- [Drizzle adapter](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/database/drizzle) — Use Drizzle ORM with Postgres, MySQL, SQLite, or Cloudflare D1.\n-- [Database adapters](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/database) — Wire authfn to your database — Drizzle, raw Postgres, SQLite, in-memory, or a custom adapter.\n-- [Memory adapter](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/database/memory) — In-memory database — for tests and local development. Don't use it in production.\n-- [Postgres adapter](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/database/postgres) — Use Postgres with authfn through the supported Drizzle adapter path.\n-- [SQLite adapter](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/database/sqlite) — Single-file local database — perfect for local-first apps and self-hosted single-node deployments.\n-- [Adapters](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters) — Database, mail, and OAuth — bring your own. authfn ships only the contracts.\n-- [Custom mail adapter](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/mail/custom) — The delivery contract is one method — drop in any mailer.\n-- [Mail adapters](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/mail) — authfn never bundles a mail provider. Implement the delivery contract for the provider you already use.\n-- [Postmark](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/mail/postmark) — Use Postmark as your authfn email provider.\n-- [Resend](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/mail/resend) — Use Resend as your authfn email provider.\n-- [SendGrid](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/mail/sendgrid) — Use SendGrid as your authfn email provider.\n-- [AWS SES](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/mail/ses) — Use AWS SES as your authfn email provider.\n-- [OAuth adapters](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/oauth) — Bundled OAuth providers and how to extend.\n-- [Admin](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/admin) — @authfn/admin — admin-only routes for listing and deleting users, with a customizable authorization callback.\n-- [Claude](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/claude) — One-click Claude Desktop / Claude Code setup for authfn.\n-- [Codex / Droid](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/codex) — AGENTS.md / Factory Codex / Factory Droid setup for authfn.\n-- [Cursor](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/cursor) — One-click Cursor setup for authfn — MCP, llms.txt, and rules.\n-- [AI Resources](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources) — First-class integrations for LLM-based coding assistants — llms.txt, an MCP server, and per-tool skill packs.\n-- [llms.txt](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/llms-txt) — Static context files generated from the docs — drop into your assistant for one-shot RAG.\n-- [MCP server](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/mcp) — A Model Context Protocol server that exposes authfn docs, OpenAPI, and example apps as live tools for your assistant.\n-- [Skills](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/skills) — Packaged authfn patterns your assistant can apply on request — sign-in flows, OAuth, multi-region, and more.\n-- [API Reference](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/api) — HTTP endpoints exposed by authfn, generated from your enabled plugins.\n-- [Account linking](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/account-linking) — When authfn merges multiple sign-in methods into a single user, and how to control that policy.\n-- [Architecture](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/architecture) — How the authfn kernel, plugins, hooks, runtime resolver, and database adapter fit together.\n-- [Cookies](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/cookies) — Names, prefixes, domain, SameSite, Secure, path, and max-age — every knob authfn exposes for cookie-based sessions.\n-- [CSRF protection](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/csrf) — How authfn defends against cross-site request forgery, what's protected, what's exempt, and how to extend the policy.\n-- [Envelopes](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/envelopes) — Every authfn HTTP response wraps either a success payload or a structured error in a stable envelope shape.\n-- [Errors](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/errors) — Every error code authfn can return — what it means, what HTTP status it maps to, whether it's retryable, and what the client should show.\n-- [Hooks](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/hooks) — Lifecycle callbacks that let you intercept and augment authfn flows — before/after user creation, session issuance, OTP send, OAuth start/callback, and account deletion.\n-- [Core Concepts](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts) — The mental model behind authfn — kernel, plugins, sessions, cookies, runtime, regions, observability, and the canonical envelope/error contract.\n-- [Observability](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/observability) — Every meaningful action in authfn emits a structured event. Wire one callback and you have audit, metrics, and tracing.\n-- [OpenAPI](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/openapi) — How authfn generates and serves an OpenAPI 3.1 document of your enabled plugin set.\n-- [Plugins](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/plugins) — How authfn plugins compose into a single auth runtime — lifecycle, ordering, configuration, schema, and authoring custom plugins.\n-- [Rate limiting](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/rate-limiting) — What authfn does and doesn't enforce, and how to add the limits you need.\n-- [Multi-region routing](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/regions) — Direct regional authorities and canonical-gateway routing for region-pinned AuthFn identity data.\n-- [Runtime resolver](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/runtime) — How authfn decides per-request issuer, base URL, OAuth credentials, and cookie domain — and how to take control of that decision.\n-- [Security](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/security) — authfn's threat model, what it defends against, what it expects you to handle, and how to harden a deployment.\n-- [Sessions](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/sessions) — How authfn issues, verifies, rotates, and revokes sessions — for cookie-based browsers and bearer-token clients alike.\n-- [account-settings](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples/account-settings) — Post-login UX — two-factor enroll/disable, API-key list/create/revoke, and protected-endpoint authentication via API key.\n-- [Examples](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples) — Five runnable example apps that exercise authfn end-to-end with deterministic Playwright tests.\n-- [multi-region-routing](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples/multi-region-routing) — Two regional authfn servers and a single browser client — region lookup, wrong-authority correction, and successful continuation.\n-- [otp-recovery](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples/otp-recovery) — Verify-email OTP, OTP sign-in, password reset via OTP, with a deterministic demo inbox for Playwright assertions.\n-- [password-sessions](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples/password-sessions) — Email/password sign-up, sign-in, current-session fetch, multi-device session listing, single-session revoke, and sign-out.\n-- [social-oauth](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples/social-oauth) — OAuth start, callback, and disconnect — with a local fake provider so the example doesn't need real third-party credentials.\n-- [Bun](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/bun) — Use Bun's native HTTP server — authfn is WHATWG-native, no adapter needed.\n-- [Bring your own framework](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/byo) — Mount authfn anywhere that hands you a Request — including bare http.createServer, Deno.serve, and AWS Lambda.\n-- [Express](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/express) — Mount authfn on Express with @superfunctions/http-express.\n-- [FastAPI](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/fastapi) — Mount the Python authfn kernel on FastAPI.\n-- [Flask](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/flask) — Mount the Python authfn kernel on Flask.\n-- [Hono](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/hono) — Mount authfn on Hono with @superfunctions/http-hono.\n-- [Frameworks](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks) — Adapters that mount the authfn router on the HTTP framework you already use.\n-- [Next.js (App Router)](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/nextjs) — Mount authfn as a Route Handler and read sessions from RSC, Server Actions, and middleware.\n-- [Starlette](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/starlette) — Starlette apps mount Python authfn through FastAPI — there is no dedicated Starlette extra.\n-- [SvelteKit](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/sveltekit) — Mount authfn as a SvelteKit endpoint and read sessions from server load functions, hooks, and form actions.\n-- [Getting Started](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/getting-started) — Declare an authfn app, create its server runtime, mount it on Hono, and sign in from the typed client.\n-- [Welcome to authfn](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs) — Self-hosted, framework-agnostic authentication for TypeScript and Python. Sessions, OTP, passwords, social OAuth, API keys, 2FA, multi-region — opt in to what you need.\n-- [API keys plugin](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/api-keys) — User-owned and server-issued unowned API keys — hashed, scoped, revocable.\n-- [Authoring custom plugins](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/authoring) — Step-by-step guide to writing your own authfn plugin — schema, routes, hooks, observability, and OpenAPI integration.\n-- [Email OTP plugin](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/email-otp) — One-time codes for email verification, magic-link sign-in, sign-up, and password reset.\n\n[2941 more lines in file. Use offset=251 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791366457546}}
